course: dictionary
lesson: 13452
title: Householder
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A householder is a person who owns or rents a house and is responsible for it. Look at the two parts of the word: house and holder. A holder is someone who holds or looks after something, so a householder looks after the house.
explain: «The council has written to all householders about the new bin days.» The letter goes to every person who owns or rents a house. Ask yourself: who is responsible for the house? That person is the householder.
explain: In a money problem, «The householder pays the rent of £600 each month.» The context tells you the job: paying rent, bills or a mortgage. Check your meaning by swapping in «the person who owns or rents the house», and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: A householder owns or rents a house.
fact: A householder is responsible for the house.
fact: «Householders» is the plural, which means more than one householder.
fact: «Local householders» means the people who own or rent houses in the area nearby.
fact: Householder is a noun, so it names a person.
exercise: «The council has written to all householders.» Who are the householders? | Children at school | * People who own or rent a house | People who build roads | Shopkeepers in the high street
exercise: Find and copy the word that means more than one householder. | council | * householders | household | holder
exercise: The word householder is made from house and holder. What is a householder? | * A person who looks after and is responsible for a house | A person who builds new houses | A house that holds lots of people | A tool for holding open doors
test: «The householder paid the rent on Friday.» What does householder mean? | A shop that sells houses | A friend who visits for tea | * Someone who owns or rents a house and is responsible for it | A builder who fixes roofs
test: «Local householders were asked to save water.» Who was asked? | Doctors at the hospital | * The people who own or rent houses nearby | Teachers in the town hall | Children on the school bus
exam: Which sentence uses householder correctly? | «The householder sang a song at school.» | «The householder is a small, bright fish.» | «The householder plays football in the park.» | * «Every householder must keep their home in good repair.»
